<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04TX">Remark <span data-tag="04TX">10.2.0.9</span>.</a> In §<a data-tag="04WG" href="/tag/04WG">10.2.2</a>, we adopt a slightly different definition of quotient morphism (Definition <a data-tag="04WH" href="/tag/04WH">10.2.2.1</a>), which makes sense in any $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (that is, we do not need to assume that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ admits pullbacks). Our definition is formulated using the language of <em>sieves</em>, which we review in §<a data-tag="04TZ" href="/tag/04TZ">10.2.1</a>. When $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ admits pullbacks, the sieve-theoretic definition reduces to the requirement that $\operatorname{\check{C}}_{\bullet }(X/Y)$ is a colimit diagram (see Proposition <a data-tag="04WL" href="/tag/04WL">10.2.2.4</a>). </p>
